<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad ibn Tughj al-Ikhshid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">882–946</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as al-Ikhshid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad ibn Tughj al-Ikhshid was a Turkic commander of Fergana origin who became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 935 and founded the Ikhshidid dynasty. In 939 the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> granted him the old Ferghanan princely title of ikhshid. He restored order in Egypt, held off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> and extended his rule over southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, leaving power at his death to his sons under the regent Kafur.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-tughj-al-ikhshid.jpg" alt="Muhammad ibn Tughj al-Ikhshid" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Cplakidas (uploader) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinar_of_Muhammad_al-Ikhshid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
